  "enhanced_information": "In an 84-year-old patient with a long-standing ulcerated tumor lesion on the scalp, the most likely diagnosis is basal cell carcinoma (BCC). BCC is the most common skin cancer, particularly in older adults, and is characterized by slow growth and a tendency to ulcerate, especially in chronic lesions. The absence of locoregional adenopathies supports a localized skin cancer diagnosis, further aligning with BCC. While squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) and other malignancies are possible, the chronic nature and typical presentation of the lesion are more consistent with BCC. Evidence regarding scalp lesions indicates that ulceration is a common manifestation in BCC, reinforcing this diagnosis as the most probable option."
